Video 1
    Why docker?
Docker is more light weight and provides the same functionality as a virtual machine.
    Virtual Machines                             Docker
1)Occupies alot of memory space              Docker containers occupy less space on the host machine
on the host machine.
2)Long boot-up time                          Short boot-up time.
3)Running multiple virtual machines          Containers have a better performance as they
leads to unstable performance                are hosted in a single Docker engine.
4)Difficult to scale up                      Easy to scale up.
5)Low efficiency                             High efficiency
6)Compatibility issues while porting         Easy portable across different platforms
across different platforms
7)Data volumes cannot be shared              Data volumes can be shared and reused among multiple containers

DevOps and its tools
    What is DevOps
This is a collaboration between development and operation teams which enables continuous delivery of applications and services to our end users.
Developers are interested in
-Planning
-Codebase
-Building
-Testing
while Operations are to efficiently
-Deploy
-Operate
-Monitor
For planning and codebase, we use tools like git or jira
For Building, we use Gradel and Maven
For Testing we use Selenuim(Se)
The integration between dev and ops, we use Jenkins
The deployment Operation is done with Docker and chef
For Monitoring, we use Nagios

    What is Docker?
Docker is a tool which is used to automate the deployment of applications in lightweight containers so
that the applications can work efficiently in different environments.
The container is a software package that consist with dependecies requred to run the application.
    Docker container Features
-Multiple containers run on the same hardware
-The containers are Maintained in isolated environment
-They are highly productive
-They are quick and easy to configure
Example
1)lets take an example where you plan to rent a house in Airbnb
But in the house, there are 3 rooms and only one cupboard and kitchen.
-Problem: non of the guest wants to share cupboard and kitchen.
Because every individual has different peferences when it comes to the cupboard and kitchen usage
-solution: we put a kitchen and cupboard in eachroom
2)lets use the example with computers, where all the three applications use different frameworks
What if i want to run all my applications with their suitable frameworks?
This is where docker will help you run the applications with their different frameworks
solution: Docker provides the suitable frameworks for each suitable application
Since Every Appllication has a framework with a suitable version,
this space also can be utilized for a new software application along with its required framework.

How does Docker work
Docker is the base engine installed on your host machine to build and run containers using docker components and services.
-Docker uses a client-server architecture
There is a client installed on the hardware and
there is a client that conatins the docker product.
there is a server that controls how the docker client is created
-Docker client and server communicate using Rest Api
-Docker client is a service which runs a command
The command is translated using REST API and is sent to the Docker Daemon(Server)
-Then, Docker Daemon checks the client request and interacts with the operating system in order to create or manage containers

What are the components of Docker?
The four main components the comprises the docker engine
1)Docker client and server
2)Docker images
3)Docker containers
4)Docker registry

Docker client and server
-Docker client is accessed from the terminal(power shell) and docker Host runs the Docker Daemon and registry
-A user can build Docker images and run Docker containers by passing commands from the Docker client to Docker Server

Docker images
-Docker image is a template with instructions, which is used for creating Docker containers.
-A Docker image is built using a file called Docker file
-once you have created that file, the docker image is stored in a Docker Hub or in a repository(like registry.hub.Docker.com)
    Syntax to create a Docker Container using Docker image
Docker container create [OPTIONS] IMAGE [COMMAND] [ARG]
-you are creating an option of a new container
-you are identifying what the image will look like
-what are the commands needed and the arguements in those commands

Docker containers
-The Docker container is a standalone executable software package which includes applications and their dependecies.
-Docker is so light weight that Numerous Docker Containers run on the same infrastructure and share operating system(os) with its other container.
-Here, each application runs in isolation

Docker registry
Docker registry is an open source server-side service used for hosting and distributing images.
-Docker also has its own default registry called Docker Hub
-Here, images can be stored in either public or private repositories.
public repositories can be used to host Docker images which can be used by everyone.
private repositories allows a user to store Docker images that he/she wants to keep priavte.
-Pull and push are the commands used by users in order to intreact with a Docker registry
In order to build a container, pull command is used to get a Docker image from the Docker repository
    syntax
Docker pull <image_name>:<tags associated with the image>
This pulls an image from Docker Repository
-With push command, a user can store the Docker image in Docker registry
    syntax
Docker push <image_name>:<tag>
This pushes an image to Docker Repository

Recap
-Docker file creates a Docker image using the build command
-A docker image contains all the docker project's code
-Using the Docker image, any user can run the code in order to create Docker Containers
-once a Docker image is built, its uploaded in a registry or a Docker Hub which can be shared across your entire team
-From the Docker Hub, users can get the Docker image and build new containers

Video 2
What is docker
This is a way to package software so it can run on any hardware.
Three things you must know to understand how the process works
-Dockerfile: This is a blue print for building a docker image.
-Image: This is a template for running a docker container
-Container: This is a running process
The developer that created the software can define the environment with a docker file
Then any developer can use the docker file rebuild the environment which is saved as an imutable snapshot know as an image
Images can be uploaded to the cloud and be made both public and private registries
And any developer that wants to run that software can pull the image down to create a container(which is just the running process of that image)
In other words, one image file can be used to spon the same process multiple times in multiple places
    instructions to build a docker image
Download docker extensions for your IDE(optional)
-create a docker file in the root of the project
The dockerfile contains code to build the docker image and ultimately run your app as a container
-The first instruction in our docker file is 'FROM'.
-The next is the official node js image(node:12).
-Add the working directory
-Add our app source code(we use the 'COPY' instruction which takes two arguments)
The package.json location and where we want to copy it in the contianer(The working diretory)
-Then we RUN(it is just like opening the therminal and dong npm install)
after that is finished, the results will be commit to the docker image as a layer
since we now have our modules in the image by doing the first COPY, we can now copy over our source code by
-COPY . . (this copies all our local files to the working directory)
we dont want to copy the node modules, so we create a docker ignore file to ignore the node modules
-create a ".dockerignore file" and add the node_modules to it. this works like a .gitignore file
in order to run our code, we are using an environment variable, we can set the environment variable in the container using
-ENV PORT=8080 (This is so we can access our express app publicly)
-CMD [ "npm", "start" ]
There can only be one of the above per docker file and it tells the container how to run the actual application
    command to build a docker image
-docker build -t steverado9/demoapp:1.0 .
This will give your image a name tag that is easy to remember,
t=tag, steverado9=name on your dockerhub, 1.0=version, .=path to the dockerfile which is the current working directory
After we have sucessfully built the image,
we can use it as a base image to create other images or we can use it to run containers
To use this image in the real word,
We push it to a container registry somewhere(dockerhub or your favourite cloud provider)
The command to do that is
-docker push
and a developer somewhere else can do docker pull to pull the image.
Since we are just running it locally on our system, we do this on our termianl
-docker run image Id
This will create a running process called a container
-we see app listening to local host 8080
but if we go to local host 8080, we wont see anything
    why cant we access our container locally
by default, it is not accessable to the outside world
we have to refactor our command in our docker file
by using the p flag to implement port forwarding from the docker container to our local machine
so we run,
docker run -p 5000:8080 Image id

When you stop a continer, any data created inside of it will be lost
    How to share data between multiple containers
This can be done with volumes
A volume is a dedicated folder on the host machine and inside this folder,
a container can create files that can be re-mounted into future containers or multiple containers at the same time
to create a volume, we use
-docker volume create folder_name
-docker run / --mount source=folder_name
now multiple containers can mount this container and access the same set of files and the files stick around after the containers are shutdown

    Debugging
When you go to the logs of a container, you can click on the logs

    Docker compose
This is a tool for running multiple docker containers at the same time
Example
Lets image our node app wants to access mySQL database, and we want a volume to persit the database accross multiple containers.
-we can manage all that by creating a "docker-compose.yml file" in the root of our project
-Inside the file, we have a services object
where each key in the object represents a different container that we want to run
-we use "web" to define our node.js app that e have already built
-We use build to point it to the current working directory where it can find the docker file
-we will also define the port forwarding configuration as well
-we have a seperate container 'db' which is our my sql database process
-we also define a volume to store the database data across multiple containers
and we can mount that volume in our db container
after we have this configuration set, we can run this in the command line
-docker-compose up
we will find this file and run all the containers together
run
-docker-compose down
to shut down all the containers together

video 3
    What is Docker?
Docker is a platform for building, running and shipping applications in a consistent manner.
With docker, we can easily package our application with everything it needs and run it anywhere.

    Virtual Machines vs Containers
-A virtual machine is an abstraction of a machine(physical hardware).
meaning A VM is a pretend computer inside your real computer.

-A container is an isolated environment for running an application.
containers
-Allow running multiple apps in isolation
-Are lightweight
-use operating system of the host
-starts quickly

    Architecture of Docker
Docker uses a client server architecture
It has a client component that talks to a server component using REST api
The server aka Docker engine sits on the background and takes care of building and running docker containers.
A container share the kernel of the host computer.
The kernel is the core of an operating system(it manages applications and hardware resources)
Every os has its own karnel and each karnel has different apis
that is why you cannot run a linux application on a mac and a windows application on a mac
cos under the hood this application needs to talk to the karnel of this os.

    Development Workflow when using docker
-We take an application and dockerize it
-This is done by creating a dockerfile
-A dockerfile contains a set of instructions that docker uses to package up the application into an image.
-This image contains everything an application needs to run.
-Once we have an image, we tell docker to start a container using that image.
-A container is a process but it is a special type of process cos it contains its own file system controlled by the image.
-We can push the image to a registry like "docker hub"
-Docker hub is a storage for images that anyone can use
-When our application is on docker hub, we can pull it on any machine running docker and we can start our application.
    A typical development workflow
steps
cd to Desktop
1)mkdir hello-docker
create a folder called hello-docker
2)cd hello-docker
change directory to hello-docker
3)code .
open vs code
4)create a new file "app.js" and write a javascript code
5)use "node filename" to see the code in the terminal
6)create a "Dockerfile"
7)FROM base_image
what is a base_image
you can find base_images in dockerhub, so dockerhub is a registry for base_images
"FROM node:alpine"
alpine: This is a small size of base image that we are going to be using for this tutorial
8)We need to copy our application (program files) into the image directory called app
COPY . /app
9)The current working directory in the image
WORKDIR /app
9)we use the command instruction to execute a command
CMD node app.js
10) we go to the terminal and run to build our image
docker build -t hello-docker .
-t=tag hello-docker=tagname .=current directory
11) to see all the images in our computer
-docker images or docker image ls. ls = short for list
12)now we can run this image on any machine running docker
since we are using our local machine,
-docker run Repository(hello-docker)

    Linux command line
Linux distribution/linux distros
linux is an open source software and many individuals and community have created their own version of linux called linux distributions.
Each of this distributions is made to fix specialized needs
Examples of Distros
Ubuntu
Debian
Alpine
Fedora
centOs etc
    Running Linux(Ubuntu)
linux is a case sensitive operating system
-docker run ubuntu
if we have this image locally, docker is going to start a container with this image.
Other wise it is going to pull this image behind the scene and then start a container.
-it stopped running the container, so we are going to run it in interactive mode
docker run -it ubuntu
-when we run this we get a shell prompt (root@23b88816f598:/#)
-The shell is a program that takes our commands and passes them to operating system(kernel) for execution
root=currently logged in user which has the highest privilleges
after the @ which is "23b88816f598" is generated by docker which is the name of the machine
/=where we are in the file system(root directory), highest directory in the file system.
#=i have the highest privilleges cos i have been logged in as root user
$=normal user
-using the 'history' command, we can see all the commands we have executed lately
-'apt' this is a package manger in ubuntu which stands for advanced package tool
-apt list : to see all the packages in the database
-apt update: this is used to update the database
-apt install nano
lets install 'nano': This is a basic text editor for linux
note: before installing a package, always run apt update to update the package database and then run apt install
type nano to see the text editor
-ctrl l, to clear the terminal window
-to remove nano
-apt remove nano

    linux file system
In linux everything is a file




docker commands
docker ps
to see the list of running processes/containers

docker ps -a
we can see stopped containers

docker run -it
to start a container and intract with it(starting a container in interactive mode)

